SLIDES = {1: {'id': 1, 'type': 'title_slide', 'title': 'Real-Time Object Detection and Tracking', 'text': ['By', 'Capt. Eng. Ahmed Gouda Ismail', 'Under Supervision of', 'Dr. Tarek Ahmed Mahmoud', 'Dr. Mohamed S. Mohamed', 'Military Technical College', 'Electrical Engineering Branch', 'Computer Engineering and Artificial Intelligence Department'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_01.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide1.xml'}, 2: {'id': 2, 'type': 'image_backed', 'title': 'PRESENTATION STRUCTURE', 'text': ['Outline', 'I', 'Introduction and Motivation', '- Detection and tracking basics - Evaluation metrics', 'slides 3 - 16', 'II', 'Related Work: Detectors and Trackers', '- Detector families and YOLO - Tracker families and ByteTrack', 'slides 17 - 28', 'III', 'Problem Formulation and Research Gap', '- The fixed-threshold problem - Research question', 'slides 29 - 31', 'IV', 'Proposed Framework: AC-MOT', '- AC-MOT pipeline - Scene Complexity Index and calibrator', 'slides 32 - 44', 'V', 'Experimental Setup: Dataset and Protocol', '- VisDrone dataset - Protocol and fair comparison', 'slides 45 - 53', 'VI', 'Results and Ablation Study', '- Main results - Ablation and speed', 'slides 54 - 59', 'VII', 'Implementation, Scale and Reproducibility', '- Implementation scale - Reproducibility', 'slides 60 - 63', 'VIII', 'Scope, Conclusion and Future Work', '- Contribution and scope - Future work', 'slides 64 - 68', '2'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_02.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide2.xml'}, 3: {'id': 3, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I', 'text': ['Introduction and Motivation', 'I', 'What object detection and multi-object tracking are, where they are used, what makes them difficult, and how their quality is measured.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_03.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide3.xml'}, 4: {'id': 4, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - FUNDAMENTALS', 'text': ['Object Detection: Definition and Output', '4', 'A single video frame. Every object the detector found is enclosed in a box and given an identifier.', 'OBJECT DETECTION Examining a single frame and answering: what objects are present, and where?', 'The detector returns three things ▸ A bounding box - the location ▸ A class label - pedestrian, car, bus ▸ A confidence score - certainty, from 0 to 1', 'E X A M P L E Look at the frame on the left. Each coloured rectangle is one detection: the box gives the position, the label gives the class, and behind each one sits a confidence score the detector assigned. The detector produced all of that from this single frame alone - it has no memory of the frame before it.', 'The detector used throughout this work is YOLOv8n, applied to every frame to locate pedestrians, cars, vans, trucks and buses.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_04.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide4.xml'}, 5: {'id': 5, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - FUNDAMENTALS', 'text': ['Multi-Object Tracking: Following IDs Across Frames', '5', 'Frame t: the person beside the white car carries identity 1.', 'Frame t+k: the same person carries identity 8. This is an identity switch.', 'Tracking: keep the same ID for the same object across frames.', 'Failure: the same object receives a new ID in a later frame.', 'the person beside the white car changes from ID 1 to ID 8. The person is the same, but the tracker has lost identity continuity.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_05.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide5.xml'}, 6: {'id': 6, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - FUNDAMENTALS', 'text': ['Detection and Tracking Compared', '6', 'The same three frames processed three ways (ByteTrack, Zhang et al., 2022).', '(a) Detection alone Each frame is treated independently. Boxes carry scores of 0.9, 0.8 and 0.1, with no memory of the preceding frame. Location is known; identity is not.', '(b) Association of strong detections only Only high-scoring boxes are linked. The pedestrian who momentarily scores 0.1 is discarded and the trajectory breaks. Conservative, but real objects are lost.', '(c) Association of every detection The 0.4 and 0.1 boxes are given a second matching round against existing tracks, and the identity is preserved. The ByteTrack family - the basis of AC-MOT.', 'E X A M P L E A weak detection is frequently a genuine object that is merely small, dark or partially occluded - not noise. Discarding it is what fragments identities.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_06.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide6.xml'}, 7: {'id': 7, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - APPLICATION DOMAINS', 'text': ['Applications of Multi-Object Tracking', '7', 'Traffic management: road footage counts vehicles and keeps every vehicle ID stable.', 'Security and surveillance: the animated scene follows people across frames.', 'Retail, robotics, healthcare: track customers, mobile robots, and patient movement.', 'Different applications, same need: detect each object, then keep its identity stable while it moves.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_07.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide7.xml'}, 8: {'id': 8, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - MOTIVATION', 'text': ['The Challenges of Real-Time Tracking', '8', 'A difficult frame: dense crowd, heavy occlusion, poor light. Identities are hard to hold.', 'An easy frame: few objects, large, well separated, good light.', 'What makes a scene hard ▸ Occlusion - objects hide one another, then return ▸ Illumination - low light and shadows destroy contrast ▸ Scale - the same object can be huge or a few pixels', 'And what it costs ▸  Difficult objects score only 0.10 - 0.35 ▸  That is the same range as background noise ▸  So the tracker loses them and re-assigns identities', 'E X A M P L E Both frames above come from the same kind of system. One is trivial and one is difficult - and a single detector setting has to serve both. That is the motivation of our work.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_08.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide8.xml'}, 9: {'id': 9, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - EVALUATION METRICS', 'text': ['How We Will Evaluate Tracking Quality', '9', 'Before the detailed slides, we ask six simple questions:', 'Detection Metrics', '• Are the boxes correct? IoU and TP / FP / FN • How reliable and complete are detections? Precision and Recall', 'Tracking Metrics', '• Is overall tracking accurate? MOTA • Does each object keep its identity? IDF1 and IDS • Is detection and association balanced? HOTA', 'Performance Metric', '• Is it fast enough for live use? FPS', 'Key message: no single metric is enough. We read detection, identity, and speed together.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_09.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide9.xml'}, 10: {'id': 10, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - EVALUATION METRICS', 'text': ['IoU and Non-Maximum Suppression (NMS)', '10', 'Green denotes ground truth, red the prediction. Agreement increases from left to right.', 'IoU Area of overlap divided by area of union. 0 indicates no overlap; 1 indicates identical boxes.', 'Interpretation ▸  IoU below 0.5 → counted as incorrect ▸  IoU of 0.5 or above → counted as correct ▸  0.5 is the conventional acceptance threshold', 'NMS - Remove Duplicate Detections Before NMS: one object may produce several overlapping candidate boxes, each with a different confidence score. Why: the detector sees the same object from multiple locations and feature levels. After NMS: IoU identifies duplicates; keep the highest-confidence box only. Simple rule: NMS uses IoU to find duplicate boxes and keeps the highest-confidence box.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_10.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide10.xml'}, 11: {'id': 11, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - EVALUATION METRICS', 'text': ['TP, FP, FN and TN: Detection Outcomes', '11', 'FALSE POSITIVE: a detection in the wrong location (IoU 0.22).', 'FALSE NEGATIVE: the object is present but was not reported.', 'TP - True Positive A real object was detected correctly.', 'FP - False Positive The detector reported an object that was not really there.', 'FN - False Negative A real object was present, but the detector missed it .', 'E X A M P L E A real bird is in the image. If the detector boxes it correctly, it is TP. If it draws a box on empty background, it is FP. If it misses the bird, it is FN.', 'TN - True Negative: No object was present, and the detector correctly reported nothing.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_11.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide11.xml'}, 12: {'id': 12, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - EVALUATION METRICS', 'text': ['Precision and Recall', '12', 'A surveillance frame: four people have been boxed and given identities.', 'PRECISION How many detections were correct? Precision = TP / (TP + FP)', 'RECALL How many real objects were found? Recall = TP / (TP + FN)', 'TP = 4   FP = 1   FN = 1', 'Precision = 4/5 = 0.80      Recall = 4/5 = 0.80', 'Precision and Recall use different denominators.', 'E X A M P L E Precision: The detector reported 10 objects, and 8 of them were correct. Therefore, the precision is 80%. Recall: There were 10 real objects in the scene, and the detector found 8 of them. Therefore, the recall is 80%.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_12.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide12.xml'}, 13: {'id': 13, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - EVALUATION METRICS', 'text': ['Identity Switches (IDS)', '14', 'Before occlusion: identity 4.', 'After occlusion: the same person, now identity 8 - one identity switch.', 'IDENTITY SWITCH The tracker assigns a new identity to an object it has already been following. Lower is better;', 'E X A M P L E A car has ID 4 in one frame and ID 8 in the next frame. That is one identity switch.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_13.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide13.xml'}, 14: {'id': 14, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - EVALUATION METRICS', 'text': ['MOTA - Multiple Object Tracking Accuracy', '13', 'MOTA = 1 - (FP + FN + IDS) / GT MOTA measures overall tracking accuracy by combining false positives, missed objects, and identity switches into one score. Higher is better.', 'Four people are being tracked in this frame, carrying identities 2, 3, 4 well tracked and 5 out of scope.', 'Interpretation ▸  1.00: perfect tracking, no errors. ▸  0.00: number of errors is about equal to the number of objects. ▸  Negative values: errors are more than the number of objects. ▸ 0.20: can still be a meaningful result on very difficult scene.', 'E X A M P L E There are 4 real objects, with 1 false positive, 1 missed object, and 1 identity switch. MOTA = 1 - 3/4 = 0.25.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_14.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide14.xml'}, 15: {'id': 15, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - EVALUATION METRICS', 'text': ['IDF1 - Identity F1 Score', '15', 'Outer rings denote ground-truth trajectories; inner markers denote predictions. IDF1 measures how much of each trajectory retained a single identity.', 'IDF1 How well the tracker keeps the same correct ID for each object over time.', 'Why it is necessary ▸  MOTA can still look good even when IDs change. ▸  IDF1 shows exactly this problem . ▸  IDF1 of 80% means the tracker kept the correct ID 80% of the time.', 'E X A M P L E A car keeps the correct ID in 8 of 10 frames. Its IDF1 is 80%.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_15.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide15.xml'}, 16: {'id': 16, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION I - EVALUATION METRICS', 'text': ['HOTA* and Processing Speed', '16', 'HOTA*: internal proxy combining detection and association terms; it is not official HOTA. Higher is better. Detection: did we find the right objects? Association: did each object keep the same ID across frames? Example: strong detection with changing IDs still gives a low HOTA.', 'FPS - frames per second The number of frames processed each second. Live video runs at 25 to 30 frames per second, so anything slower falls behind reality. Recorded full-17 run: A3 = 28.9 FPS; A0 = 30.8 FPS on T4, actual FP32.', 'E X A M P L E - FPS A camera sends 25 frames every second: A3 runs at 28.9 FPS -> real-time for a 25 FPS stream. A0 runs at 30.8 FPS -> the adaptive cost is only 6.3%.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_16.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide16.xml'}, 17: {'id': 17, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION II', 'text': ['Related Work: Detectors and Trackers', 'II', 'The state of the art from our IEEE ICMISI 2026 survey: detector and tracker families, benchmarks, datasets, and the trade-off landscape that exposed the research gap.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_17.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide17.xml'}, 18: {'id': 18, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION II - DETECTORS', 'text': ['Methodological Taxonomy of Detectors', '18', '1. One-stage detectors Directly predict boxes and classes. YOLO, SSD, RetinaNet.', '2. Two-stage detectors Propose regions, then refine them. Faster R-CNN, Mask R-CNN.', '3. Transformer detectors Use attention for global context. DETR, Deformable DETR, RT-DETR.', 'Why we choose family 1 : - Small compute - Real-time speed footprint - Confidence can be calibrated - Pairs well with ByteTrack in Real-time', 'E X A M P L E We choose the detector family first because every tracker receives its boxes from it. A slow detector or missed object will limit any tracker.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_18.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide18.xml'}, 19: {'id': 19, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION II - DETECTORS', 'text': ['The Architecture of a YOLO Detector', '19', 'Backbone Reads the raw picture and turns it into features: edges, then shapes, then objects. Like your eye recognising shapes.', 'Neck Mixes the detailed early layers with the abstract deep ones, so tiny AND large objects both survive. Critical when objects are only a few pixels wide.', 'Head Writes the final answer: box, class, confidence for every object found. This is where conf comes from.', 'E X A M P L E Trace the diagram left to right. The same frame passes through all three stages: the backbone turns pixels into features, the neck merges fine and coarse detail so a small object is not lost, and the head emits the boxes and the confidence scores used on the earlier slides .'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_19.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide19.xml'}, 20: {'id': 20, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION II - DETECTORS', 'text': ['State-of-the-Art Detectors: Benchmark Comparison', '20', 'All numbers on COCO val2017, T4 GPU, TensorRT FP16, 640x640, batch 1 - so they are directly comparable.', 'E X A M P L E Compare the first row with the highlighted one. Faster R-CNN and YOLOv8n reach almost the same accuracy, 37.0 against 37.3 mAP - but one needs 172 ms per frame and the other 3.2 ms. Identical accuracy, 54 times the speed, That comparison alone is why the highlighted row was chosen.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_20.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide20.xml'}, 21: {'id': 21, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION II - DETECTORS', 'text': ['Reasons for Choosing YOLOv8n', '21', '37.3 mAP on COCO', '3.2 ms per frame on a T4', '3.2M parameters - the smallest', '~300 FPS of headroom left', 'Why it fits ▸  Fast enough for real-time use on one mid-range GPU. ▸  Small enough o run on an embedded computer. ▸  Leaves extra speed for tracking and larger image sizes. ▸  Accurate enough for the calibrator to adjust the settings effectively.', 'Why NOT the highest-mAP model ▸  The main idea is the adaptive system, not using a bigger detector. ▸  Keeping one fixed detector makes the comparison fair. ▸  Bigger YOLO models need more time and memory. ▸  The same calibrator can be used with larger YOLO models later.', 'E X A M P L E Every result later in this talk compares five ablation systems. If each ran a different detector, a rise in MOTA could simply mean one system had a stronger detector. Fixing YOLOv8n across A0-A4 removes that explanation entirely: whatever changes must have come from the tracking architecture.', 'This is the single most important control in the study: all five compared systems run the identical YOLOv8n, with no retraining anywhere.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_21.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide21.xml'}, 22: {'id': 22, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION II - TAXONOMY', 'text': ['Methodological Taxonomy of Trackers', '22', '1. Tracking-by-detection Two separate parts: a detector finds boxes, then a separate tracker links them across frames. SORT, DeepSORT, ByteTrack. This is our family.', '2. Joint detection + ReID One single network does both jobs at once: it outputs boxes and an appearance fingerprint together. FairMOT, JDE, CenterTrack.', '3. Transformer trackers Attention looks at the whole frame at once and removes anchors, NMS and Kalman assumptions. DETR, MOTR, TrackFormer.', 'The classic tracking-by-detection loop: detect, predict, match, manage tracks, output.', 'Why we chose family 1 ▸  You can swap either half independently ▸  It is fast enough to run on embedded hardware ▸  Every gain can be traced to ONE component ▸  It is interpretable - you can explain each step', 'E X A M P L E The detector and tracker are separate parts connected together.Because they are separate:Either one can be changed independently.If performance improves, we can clearly know which part caused the improvement.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_22.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide22.xml'}, 23: {'id': 23, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION II - TRACKERS', 'text': ['Evolution of Trackers: SORT to ByteTrack', '23', 'SORT (2016) Position only. Predicts where each box will go, then matches by overlap. Very fast, 143 FPS - but 4,852 ID switches.', 'DeepSORT (2017) Adds an appearance fingerprint, so a person can be recognised after hiding. IDS drops to 1,455, but slower.', 'ByteTrack (2022) Keeps the WEAK boxes too and gives them a second matching round. 80.3% MOTA, real-time - our baseline.', 'BoT-SORT (2022) Adds camera-motion compensation. Lowest ID switches, but heavy. 247 IDS at only 6.4 FPS.', 'Track management: create, update, keep alive, delete.', 'What every tracker must do ▸  Start a track when a new object appears ▸  Update it while the object is visible ▸  Keep it alive for a while when it disappears ▸  Delete it when it has been gone too long', 'E X A M P L E A person walks into view and a track is created. While they remain visible it is updated every frame. When they pass behind an obstacle the track is held alive for a set number of frames - that is the buffer. Only if they never reappear is it deleted.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_23.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide23.xml'}, 24: {'id': 24, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION II - TRACKERS', 'text': ['ByteTrack as the Baseline of This Work', '24', 'Algorithm 1   ByteTrack two-stage association', 'Require: video V, detector Det, threshold τ', 'Ensure:  set of tracks T', '1', 'T ← empty set', '2', 'for each frame f in V do', '3', 'D ← Det(f)', '4', 'D_high ← { d in D : score(d) > τ }', '5', 'D_low  ← { d in D : score(d) ≤ τ }', '6', 'predict every t in T with a Kalman filter', '7', 'associate T with D_high by IoU     (first pass)', '8', 'T_rem, D_rem ← unmatched tracks, unmatched D_high', '9', 'associate T_rem with D_low by IoU  (second pass)', '10', 'delete tracks unmatched for too long', '11', 'initialise a new track for each d in D_rem', '12', 'end for', '13', 'return T', 'The key insight ▸  Earlier trackers discarded every weak detection ▸  ByteTrack gives them a second matching round', 'Why that matters in the air ▸  A low-confidence box may still be a real small object. ▸  Keeping it helps the tracker keep the same ID preventing increasing of ID switches.', 'E X A M P L E Read the algorithm beside this text against row (c) of the ByteTrack figure shown in Section I. The first pass matches only the confident boxes. The second pass takes the tracks that found no partner and offers them the weak boxes - which is how the 0.4 and 0.1 detections keep their identity instead of being discarded.', 'AC-MOT and ByteTrack are complementary: the calibrator produces more weak-but-genuine detections in difficult scenes, and ByteTrack is the family designed to exploit them.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_24.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide24.xml'}, 25: {'id': 25, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION II - BENCHMARKS', 'text': ['Published Tracking Accuracy MOTA on MOT17', '25', 'SORT', '43.1%', 'DeepSORT', '59.8%', 'FairMOT', '67.5%', 'ByteTrack', '80.3%  <- our base', 'SMILEtrack', '81.7%', 'MOTA does not fully show identity problems. A tracker may get 80% MOTA but still give the same car different IDs several times. For surveillance, keeping the correct ID is very important.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_25.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide25.xml'}, 26: {'id': 26, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION II - BENCHMARKS', 'text': ['The Same Trackers Judged on Identity Switches', '26', 'SORT', '4,852 switches - worst', 'ByteTrack', '2,196', 'DeepSORT', '1,455', 'OC-SORT', '784 - best', 'E X A M P L E Notice the order flipped. SORT was 3rd on accuracy but is by far the worst on identity. ByteTrack has the best MOTA of the group yet only middling IDS. This is why one metric is never enough.', 'ByteTrack is NOT the lowest-IDS tracker. It was chosen for the balance: strong MOTA, genuinely real-time, and a low-confidence association pass that suits weak detections. Identity behaviour is evaluator-sensitive, so the full-17 run reports both the main evaluator and an IoU 0.50 replay diagnostic.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_26.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide26.xml'}, 27: {'id': 27, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION II - DATASETS', 'text': ['Benchmark Datasets', '27', 'E X A M P L E The three examples are all valid, but they have different difficulty levels. An object can look large in one video and very small in another. A method that works well on easy scene may fail on harder scene, so the benchmark should match the real operating conditions.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_27.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide27.xml'}, 28: {'id': 28, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION II - SYNTHESIS', 'text': ['Conclusions of the Survey', '28', '~49x lower latency Faster R-CNN (2015): 172 ms/frame RF-DETR-S (2025): 3.5 ms/frame', '+43% higher mAP Faster R-CNN (2015): 37.0 mAP RF-DETR-S (2025): 52.9 mAP', '81.7% MOTA SMILEtrack: highest in MOT17 comparison', '3.5 ms latency RF-DETR-S (2025): lowest in detector comparison', 'Survey conclusion No single model dominates all scenarios. The optimal choice is a dynamic trade-off between latency, compute, and precision.', 'Our selected baseline: After comparing several detector-tracker combinations, YOLOv8n + ByteTrack gave the best balance between accuracy, speed, and computational cost. Our main goal is to test whether the SCI-based adaptive method improves tracking while keeping the system real-time.', 'What this system evaluates: Other models may score higher in some scenarios. Our goal is to test whether the SCI-based adaptive strategy improves tracking while maintaining real-time speed and low computational cost.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_28.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide28.xml'}, 29: {'id': 29, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION III', 'text': ['Problem Formulation and Research Gap', 'III', 'Why a single fixed confidence threshold cannot hold when the scene keeps changing, and the research question this thesis answers.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_29.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide29.xml'}, 30: {'id': 30, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION III - MOTIVATION', 'text': ['The Problem: One Fixed Threshold Cannot Fit Every Scene', '30', 'DIFFICULT: crowding and occlusion.', 'DARK: low contrast and shadows.', 'EASY: few clear objects.', 'THE SIMPLE IDEA One UAV video can contain all three scenes. Each needs a different detector sensitivity.', 'E X A M P L E : A high threshold misses partly hidden people, so identities are lost. A low threshold accepts noise, so false tracks appear.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_30.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide30.xml'}, 31: {'id': 31, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV', 'text': ['Proposed Framework: AC-MOT', 'IV', 'A scene-aware control loop: the Scene Complexity Index, the Smart Calibrator, and an unmodified detector-tracker pair.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_31.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide31.xml'}, 32: {'id': 32, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION III - CONTRIBUTION', 'text': ['Research Question and Contributions', '32', '"Can adaptive, scene-aware detection thresholds reduce identity switches WITHOUT sacrificing overall accuracy - in real time, with no detector retraining?"', '1. SCI - a scene difficulty meter One number from 0 to 1, built from five cheap visual clues, computed on the CPU in under 0.2 ms. Crowd, edges, tiny objects, darkness, blur.', '2. Smart Calibrator Turns that number into three detector settings, every frame, with small and explainable moves. conf, IoU and input size.', '3. A fair test Four systems, one identical detector - so any gain must come from the architecture, never a better detector. Plus BoT-SORT as an outside reference.', '4. An honest negative We tried a grayscale crop-similarity ReID. It made things worse, so we report it as A4 and did not adopt it. Reported, not hidden.', 'E X A M P L E Nothing here is retrained. The intelligence sits in WHAT the detector is asked to do, frame by frame.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_32.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide32.xml'}, 33: {'id': 33, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV - THE PROPOSED FRAMEWORK', 'text': ['AC-MOT: What We Add to a Standard Tracker', '33', 'Video frame 1920 x 1080', 'Measure the scene how difficult is it?', 'Re-calibrate conf, IoU, imgsz', 'YOLOv8n unmodified', 'ByteTrack unmodified', 'Tracks stable identities', 'Traditional Approach A single detector configuration is selected before the video is processed and retained throughout - across easy and hard scenes alike. One configuration fits no scene particularly well.', 'The AC-MOT approach Scene difficulty is measured, and the detector configuration is adjusted every frame within bounded, validated limits. No retraining and no additional hardware.', 'E X A M P L E Recall the two frames on the previous slide. The crowded night frame and the open motorway frame were handed to the SAME detector with the SAME threshold. AC-MOT inserts one step before the detector: it looks at the frame, decides that the first is difficult and the second is easy, and hands each of them a different confidence threshold and input resolution.', 'The two shaded stages above constitute the entire contribution. Every other stage is standard and unmodified.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_33.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide33.xml'}, 34: {'id': 34, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV - THE PROPOSED FRAMEWORK', 'text': ['The AC-MOT Pipeline: What Happens for Each Frame', '34', '0.63', 'SCI right now', 'SCENE COMPLEXITY INDEX (SCI) One number between 0 and 1 that says how hard the current frame is for the detector.', 'How to read it ▸  Near 0.0  →  CLEAR: open road, few large objects, good light ▸  Around 0.5  →  MEDIUM: some crowding or some clutter ▸  Near 1.0  →  CROWDED: dense, tiny, dark or blurred', 'Computed every 10 frames on a 25% grayscale copy of the image, using only OpenCV and NumPy on the CPU - under 0.2 ms per frame, and zero GPU time taken from the detector.', 'E X A M P L E The dial on the left reads 0.63 for a frame like the crowded one shown earlier: many objects, most of them small, poor light. A frame like the open road would read below 0.2. SCI detects nothing itself - it only reports how hard the coming frame will be, so the detector can be set up for it.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_34.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide34.xml'}, 35: {'id': 35, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV - STEP 1', 'text': ['Step 1: Measure Scene Complexity (SCI)', '35', '1. CROWD - how many objects', '2. EDGES - how busy the background', '3. TINY - how small the objects', '4. NIGHT - is the frame dark', 'How SCI is calculated efficiently 1. Create a 25% grayscale copy: 1920x1080 RGB becomes about 480x270 with one brightness channel. 2. Measure crowding, tiny objects, edges, low light, and blur; combine them into SCI (0 = easy, 1 = hard). 3. SCI selects conf and imgsz for the next detector pass. Detection still uses the original frame, so GPU load stays negligible.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_35.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide35.xml'}, 36: {'id': 36, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV - STEP 1', 'text': ['SCI Equation: What Each Weight Means', '36', 'SCI_raw = clip(0.30c + 0.30t + 0.20e_norm + 0.10n + 0.05b, 0, 1) e_norm = min(e / 0.14, 1) SCI = mean(last 7 SCI_raw values)', 'Crowd + Tiny = 0.60 Crowd and tiny objects receive 0.30 each. They are the main causes of missed detections and identity switches in UAV footage, so they dominate SCI.', 'Edges = 0.20 Busy texture can create false boxes. It matters, but edge normalization prevents the background from overwhelming the object-related cues.', 'Night + Blur = 0.15 Night adds 0.10 and blur adds 0.05. Both are binary warnings, so they influence the score without controlling it alone.', 'Why these exact values Several weight combinations were evaluated on validation sequences using MOTA and IDS. This empirical combination gave the best trade-off; the weights are fixed, interpretable, and not learned by the model.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_36.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide36.xml'}, 37: {'id': 37, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV - STEP 1', 'text': ['SCI: Exact Formulas and Pseudocode', '37', 'c = min(count(B) / 30, 1)                         # crowd from previous-frame boxes t = 0 if count(B) = 0 else count(area(b) < 32*32 for b in B) / count(B) #small-object ratio e = mean(Canny(G, 50, 120)) / 255                 # edge density n = 1 if mean(G) < 80 else 0                       # night flag b = 1 if var(Laplacian(G)) < 180 else 0            # blur flag', 'Input preparation I_s = downscale(I, 0.25); G = grayscale(I_s). B is the set of boxes from the previous frame.', 'Edge normalization Before weighting, use e_norm = min(e / 0.14, 1). This prevents a very busy background from dominating SCI.', 'Combine and smooth r = 0.30c + 0.30t + 0.20e_norm + 0.10n + 0.05b. Append clip(r, 0, 1); SCI = mean(last 7 values).', 'B and G are the only inputs. The first two lines measure object-related difficulty; the last three measure visual difficulty.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_37.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide37.xml'}, 38: {'id': 38, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV - STEP 1', 'text': ['What Different SCI Scores Mean', '38', 'Confidence goes DOWN In a hard scene a weak detection is usually a real small object, not noise - so lowering the line recovers it.', 'Resolution goes UP A 5-10 pixel object needs more pixels to be detectable at all. Only hard frames pay that cost.', 'E X A M P L E Both moves attack the same failure. Lowering the confidence keeps the weak-but-genuine box that would otherwise be deleted; raising the input resolution gives that same object more pixels, so it is detected more strongly in the first place. Easy frames receive neither, and stay fast.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_38.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide38.xml'}, 39: {'id': 39, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV - STEP 2', 'text': ['Step 2: Use SCI to Choose Settings', '39', 'conf  =  clip( 0.245 − 0.050 × SCI ,  0.19 , 0.28 )', 'iou   =  clip( 0.490 − 0.050 × SCI ,  0.40 , 0.52 )', '(2)', 'imgsz =  832   if SCI > 0.60', '736   if SCI > 0.35', '640   otherwise', '(3)', 'Algorithm 2   Per-frame refinement by scene label', 'Require: SCI, scene label s, tiny-object ratio r', '1', 'if s ∈ {crowded, tiny, night} then  conf ← conf − 0.012', '2', 'if s = blur then  iou ← iou − 0.012', '3', 'if r > 0.50 then  imgsz ← 832', '4', 'apply the bounds of (2) again and return (conf, iou, imgsz)', 'The 0.19 floor Below it, background patches scoring 0.10-0.35 begin to enter the output as false positives.', 'Only three resolutions 640, 736, 832 - fixed steps rather than a continuous slider, giving predictable memory and latency.', 'Deliberately small moves Conservative by design: small, explainable adjustments rather than jumps that would destabilise the tracker.', 'E X A M P L E Across the entire range of SCI, from 0 to 1, the confidence moves by only 0.055 and never falls below 0.190. Small, bounded, reversible adjustments - never a jump large enough to destabilise the tracker downstream.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_39.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide39.xml'}, 40: {'id': 40, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV - STEP 2', 'text': ['Smart Calibrator: Exact Settings From SCI', '40', 'Detector side - moves every frame conf - the minimum confidence needed to keep a detection iou - the overlap threshold used by NMS to remove duplicate boxes. imgsz - the input image size given to YOLO. These values are passed directly into:model.track(conf=..., iou=..., imgsz=...)', 'Tracker side - tuned once, then frozen track_high_thresh = 0.18 track_low_thresh = 0.04 new_track_thresh = 0.20 track_buffer = 45      match_thresh = 0.86 Written into the ByteTrack YAML. Never adaptive.', 'There are two different IoU thresholds in the system: NMS IoU - used by the detector to remove duplicate boxes. The calibrator can change this value. match_thresh = 0.86 - used by ByteTrack to match detections with existing tracks. This value stays fixed.', 'What NMS actually does Lowering the NMS IoU makes suppression STRONGER - more overlapping boxes are deleted.', 'Why lower NMS IoU in hard scenes? Because we already: lower conf -> more detections are kept increase imgsz -> the detector can produce more boxes', 'E X A M P L E Lowering conf keeps more detections, which may include duplicate boxes. Lowering the NMS IoU threshold then removes those duplicates. The two settings work together.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_40.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide40.xml'}, 41: {'id': 41, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV - WHAT CHANGES', 'text': ['Exactly What AC-MOT Changes and What It Keeps', '41', 'Algorithm 3   SceneAnalyzer.analyze', 'Require: frame I, boxes B from the previous frame, history H (|H| ≤ 7)', 'Ensure:  SCI in [0,1] and a scene label s', '1', 'I_s ← downscale(I, 0.25)                       — 16x fewer pixels', '2', 'G   ← grayscale(I_s)', '3', 'beta ← mean(G)                                 — brightness', '4', 'phi  ← var(Laplacian(G))                       — sharpness', '5', 'e    ← mean(Canny(G, 50, 120)) / 255           — edge density', '6', 'c    ← min( |B| / 30 , 1 )                     — crowding', '7', 't    ← | { b in B : area(b) < 32×32 } | / |B|  — tiny ratio', '8', 'r ← 0.30c + 0.30t + 0.20 min(e/0.14, 1)', '9', 'if beta < 80  then r ← r + 0.10                — night flag', '10', 'if phi  < 180 then r ← r + 0.05                — blur flag', '11', 'append clip(r, 0, 1) to H;  drop the oldest if |H| > 7', '12', 'SCI ← mean(H)                                  — anti-flicker', '13', 's ← label by severity: night, blur, tiny, crowded, clear', '14', 'return (SCI, s)', 'Why 25% and grayscale Using a 25% grayscale copy greatly reduces computation while preserving enough information to estimate scene complexity.', 'Why the divisor 30 Beyond roughly thirty objects a frame is already saturated with crowding. The value is taken from the object counts observed in the evaluation data.', 'No learning involved OpenCV and NumPy only. No training, no data dependency, fully interpretable - and incapable of overfitting.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_41.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide41.xml'}, 42: {'id': 42, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV - DETECTOR CONTROL', 'text': ['Confidence Threshold: The Main Detector Control', '42', 'CONFIDENCE SCORE A value between 0 and 1 expressing how certain the detector is that a box contains a genuine object .', "Row (a): the detector's own scores, written above each box - 0.9, 0.8, 0.4 and 0.1.", 'High: 0.80 to 0.95 A large, clear, well-illuminated object. The detector is effectively certain.', 'Intermediate: 0.30 to 0.60 Partially occluded or small. It may be real; it may be a shadow.', 'Low: 0.10 to 0.30 Small or poorly lit. Often a real object that simply looks like noise.', 'E X A M P L E Follow the woman in the grey shirt across row (a). In frame t1 she scores 0.9 and everyone agrees she is there. In frame t2 she is partly hidden and her score falls to 0.4. With the usual threshold of 0.25 she survives - but raise it to 0.5 and she is deleted mid-video, and her identity is lost. The white pole on the right scores 0.1 in every frame and must NOT be kept.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_42.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide42.xml'}, 43: {'id': 43, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV - IMPLEMENTATION', 'text': ['Implementation: Computing SCI', '43', 'Algorithm 4   SmartCalibrator.params', 'Require: SCI, scene label s, tiny ratio r, mode flags', 'Ensure:  detector settings (conf, iou, imgsz)', '1', '(conf, iou, imgsz) ← (0.25, 0.45, 640)        — defaults', '2', 'if adaptive_threshold then', '3', 'conf ← 0.245 − 0.050 × SCI', '4', 'iou  ← 0.490 − 0.050 × SCI', '5', 'if s ∈ {crowded, tiny, night} then conf ← conf − 0.012', '6', 'if s = blur then iou ← iou − 0.012', '7', 'end if', '8', 'if adaptive_resolution then', '9', 'if SCI > 0.60 or r > 0.50 then imgsz ← 832', '10', 'else if SCI > 0.35 or s ∈ {crowded, tiny} then imgsz ← 736', '11', 'end if', '12', 'conf ← clip(conf, 0.19, 0.28)                  — false-positive floor', '13', 'iou  ← clip(iou , 0.40, 0.52)', '14', 'return (conf, iou, imgsz)', 'Everything is bounded conf in [0.19, 0.28], IoU in [0.40, 0.52], resolution restricted to three steps - bounded by construction.', 'Sixteen lines in total We intentionally kept the method very small and simple so it can run fast on limited embedded hardware.', 'E X A M P L E The method is intentionally simple. The main contribution is not complex mathematics, but making fixed detector settings adaptive to scene difficulty.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_43.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide43.xml'}, 44: {'id': 44, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION IV - IMPLEMENTATION', 'text': ['Implementation: Smart Calibrator', '44', '1. The 7-readings rolling mean Without it, SCI would jump between CROWDED and CLEAR frame by frame and the detector settings would oscillate - which hurts the tracker more than it helps. Costs nothing: a 7-value running average.', '2. Analyse only every 10 frames Scenes change over seconds, not milliseconds. Looking every 10th frame is enough and keeps the cost invisible. The result is reused for the frames in between.', '10 frames between analyses', '25% grayscale copy size', '0.2 ms per frame, CPU only', '0 GPU time taken', 'E X A M P L E The scene usually changes slowly, so checking every 10 frames is enough. The 7-reading average then keeps the SCI stable and prevents one unusual frame from changing the detector settings suddenly.', 'The recorded run measures A3 at 28.9 FPS versus A0 at 30.8 FPS: a 6.3% speed cost while remaining above the 25 FPS real-time line.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_44.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide44.xml'}, 45: {'id': 45, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION V', 'text': ['Experimental Setup: Dataset and Protocol', 'V', 'The application domain is chosen here: UAV aerial surveillance. VisDrone2019-MOT test-dev: all 17 sequences, 6,635 frames, five ablations and every scoring decision stated explicitly.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_45.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide45.xml'}, 46: {'id': 46, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION V - DOMAIN', 'text': ['Choosing the Test Domain: UAV Aerial Surveillance', '47', 'Everything so far has been general. To evaluate the method we must now commit to one domain - and we deliberately chose the hardest one available.', 'From altitude, a person is a blob about ten pixels wide.', 'The same kind of scene at night: contrast collapses.', 'And minutes later, an open road with large, easy objects.', 'Why aerial footage is the ideal test ▸  Objects are 5 to 30 pixels wide - confidence is always low ▸  The camera itself translates, rotates and changes altitude ▸  Scene difficulty swings widely inside one recording', 'Which is exactly what AC-MOT addresses ▸  A fixed threshold is at its most wrong here ▸  So the benefit of adapting it is at its most visible ▸  If the idea works anywhere, it must work here', 'E X A M P L E All three photographs above can occur within one recording. A threshold correct for the third is badly wrong for the first two - which is precisely the condition this method was built for.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_46.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide46.xml'}, 47: {'id': 47, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION V - DATA', 'text': ['The VisDrone2019-MOT Benchmark', '48', 'Why this dataset It is the only large public UAV tracking benchmark with dense per-frame labels at real aerial altitudes. 288 videos, 262,000+ frames, 14 cities.', 'E X A M P L E The three photographs above are typical VisDrone frames: a dense crowd seen from above, the same kind of scene at night, and vehicles reduced to a few pixels. These are the conditions the table beside them describes, and they are far harder than the street-level benchmarks quoted in Section II.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_47.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide47.xml'}, 48: {'id': 48, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION V - DATA', 'text': ['The 17 Recorded Evaluation Sequences', '49', 'E X A M P L E Chosen to cover the whole difficulty range - not to flatter the method. The night sequence and the densest markets are in there on purpose.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_48.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide48.xml'}, 49: {'id': 49, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION V - DATA', 'text': ['Object Classes Evaluated', '50', 'Why exactly these five ▸  They are the real targets of UAV surveillance ▸  They have enough labels for stable statistics ▸  YOLOv8n detects them reliably from altitude', 'Why the others are excluded ▸  The selection follows the VisDrone MOT evaluation protocol ▸  A bicycle at 100 m is unclear pixels making it hard to distinguish from a motorcycle, tricycle, or similar small object . ▸  Even human annotators disagree on them', 'E X A M P L E At the altitudes in this dataset a bicycle and a motorbike are both a dark smudge a few pixels across. If a human annotator cannot separate them reliably, scoring the tracker on that distinction measures annotation noise rather than tracking ability.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_49.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide49.xml'}, 50: {'id': 50, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION V - PROTOCOL', 'text': ['Ground-Truth Filtering Rules', '51', 'Why exclude the hard ones If the box is not clear enough for a human to label correctly, it is unfair to count it as a tracking error. That would measure label uncertainty, not tracker quality.', 'The annotation score field Each label has a score from 0 to 5. A score of 0 means the region should be ignored, so we remove it. We keep all labels with a score of 1 or higher.', 'E X A M P L E If an object is almost fully hidden, its box may not be clear. It is unfair to count it as an error if the tracker misses it. So we remove these cases and use the same rule for all systems.', 'Occlusion : object is hidden by another object.               &&& Truncation : Part of the object is outside the image frame.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_50.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide50.xml'}, 51: {'id': 51, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION V - PROTOCOL', 'text': ['Tracker Reset and Two Evaluation Views', '52', 'Algorithm 5   Evaluation protocol, applied to every system', 'Require: sequences S, system M, ground truth G', '1', 'for each sequence q in S do', '2', 'reset the tracker state of M      — no leakage', '3', 'G_q ← { g in G(q) : class(g) in {1,4,5,6,9},', '4', 'occ(g) < 2, trunc(g) < 2, score(g) = 1 }', '5', 'R_q ← M(q)', '6', 'main: Hungarian IoU assignment with no minimum overlap gate', '7', 'end for', '8', 'report main metrics; replay diagnostic separately uses IoU ≥ 0.50', 'MOTA  = 1 - (FP + FN + IDS) / GT', 'DetA  = TP / (TP + FP + FN)', 'AssA  = 1 - IDS / TP', 'HOTA* = √( DetA × AssA )', '(4)', 'E X A M P L E Line 3 of the algorithm is the important one. Without it, tracks still alive at the end of one sequence are carried into the next, immediately fail to match anything, and produce identity switches that belong to no video at all. The measured IDS would then describe the evaluation script rather than the tracker.', 'Hardware: Google Colab Tesla T4, batch 1. FP16 was requested, but the predictor reported actual_model_fp16=False; this recorded run is therefore labelled FP32. HOTA* is a proxy, not official HOTA.', 'E X A M P L E In tracking, the smallest evaluation decision moves the final number. Stating them explicitly is what makes the comparison reproducible.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_51.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide51.xml'}, 52: {'id': 52, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION V - PROTOCOL', 'text': ['A Fair and Attributable Experimental Design', '53', 'A0 - Baseline_Default Used with the default settings: confidence = 0.25 and image size = 640. It is the baseline used for comparison.', 'A1 - Baseline_Tuned Same detector, only the ByteTrack config corrected for UAV footage. No adaptive logic at all.', 'A2 - AdaptThresh Tuned tracker + scene-adaptive confidence and NMS IoU. Resolution remains fixed at 640.', 'A3 - AdaptResolution (ours) A2 plus adaptive input resolution. This is the adopted AC-MOT system.', 'A0-A4 run the IDENTICAL YOLOv8n detector, with no retraining anywhere. So every measured improvement has to come from the architecture - it cannot come from a bigger or better-trained detector.', 'The four systems differ in only one main way: A1 uses the corrected settings, A3 adds the adaptive layer, A3 - AdaptResolution (ours) uses a different tracking method. Everything else stays the same, including the detector, videos, ground truth, and hardware.', 'Five systems × 17 sequences = 85 recorded system-sequence runs. A3 is adopted; A4 is reported as a negative ablation.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_52.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide52.xml'}, 53: {'id': 53, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VI', 'text': ['Results and Ablation Study', 'VI', 'Full 17-sequence, five-system ablation on one detector — accuracy, identity trade-offs, speed and recorded video evidence.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_53.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide53.xml'}, 54: {'id': 54, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VI - HEADLINE', 'text': ['Full-17 Main Results — A0 to Adopted A3', '55', '+32.1% MOTA', '+21.2% IDF1', '+15.9% HOTA*', '-17.0% false negatives', '17 / 17 MOTA wins', 'E X A M P L E A3 delivers the strongest MOTA and HOTA*. A4 adds only +0.0007 IDF1, while MOTA and HOTA* fall and IDS rises by 42, so A3 remains the adopted system.', 'Unweighted mean over 17 sequences; IDS/FN/FP are summed. Legacy-v10 internal evaluator, HOTA* proxy, T4 actual FP32.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_54.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide54.xml'}, 55: {'id': 55, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VI - HEADLINE', 'text': ['Full-17 Results, Presented Graphically', '56', 'MOTA - higher is better', 'A0', '.3585', 'A1', '.3850', 'A2', '.4095', 'A3 adopted', '.4737', 'ID switches - lower is better', 'A0', '2508', 'A1', '2148', 'A2', '2136', 'A3 adopted', '2695', 'FPS - 25 and above means it can run live', 'A0', '30.8 FPS', 'A2', '29.8 FPS', 'A3 adopted', '28.9 FPS — highest accuracy', 'E X A M P L E A3 moves accuracy furthest while remaining above 25 FPS. The cost is visible too: IDS rises after adaptive resolution, so the claim is better detection and association balance — not fewer switches under every evaluator.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_55.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide55.xml'}, 56: {'id': 56, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VI - ATTRIBUTION', 'text': ['Ablation Study: Contribution of Each Component', '57', 'A0 to A1 +0.0265 MOTA and 360 fewer IDS. Tracker tuning is a clean first gain.', 'A1 to A2 +0.0245 MOTA and 12 fewer IDS. Adaptive thresholding improves recall without an identity penalty.', 'A2 to A3 +0.0642 MOTA — the largest step. It also adds 559 IDS and costs ~1 FPS; A3 is adopted with this trade-off disclosed.', 'A3 to A4 MOTA -0.0002, IDF1 +0.0007, HOTA* -0.0002 and +42 IDS. No meaningful benefit: rejected.', 'A3 is adopted because adaptive resolution provides the decisive accuracy gain. A4 is preserved as a negative ablation, not presented as the final system.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_56.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide56.xml'}, 57: {'id': 57, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VI - PER SEQUENCE', 'text': ['Per-Sequence Behaviour', '58', 'Largest gain — s0188 MOTA +0.2287; IDF1 0.3505 → 0.5359. This tiny-object case shows the value of adaptive resolution.', 'Consistency and trade-off A3 improves MOTA and IDF1 on 17/17 sequences. IDS improves on 6/17 and worsens on 11/17 under the legacy evaluator.', 'Scene coverage 12 crowded · 2 night · 2 tiny · 1 clear', 'E X A M P L E Full rows come from the recorded run. Matching videos are in presentation_videos; each video has a JSON sidecar and the folder has video_index.json.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_57.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide57.xml'}, 58: {'id': 58, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VI - SPEED', 'text': ['Accuracy–Speed Operating Point on the T4', '59', 'FPS - frames per second', 'MOTA (legacy-v10 mean)', 'live video starts here', 'A0  30.8 FPS', 'A1  30.1 FPS', 'A2  29.8 FPS', 'A3  28.9 FPS', '+32.1% MOTA vs A0', '28.9 FPS — live', "All five systems remain real-time The recorded run ranges from 28.9 to 30.8 FPS on the T4. A3's accuracy gain costs only 6.3% speed versus A0.", 'Identity result depends on the evaluation gate Legacy main: A3 IDS 2695 vs A0 2508. IoU ≥ 0.50 replay: A3 IDS 1092 vs A0 1237. Report both; do not overclaim.', 'E X A M P L E A3 is the best operating point among the tested ablations: the largest accuracy gain, still above 25 FPS, and no useful improvement from A4.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_58.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide58.xml'}, 59: {'id': 59, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VI - VIDEO EVIDENCE', 'text': ['Baseline → Full AC-MOT: Largest Visible Gain', '59', 'WHY THIS CASE', 'uav0000188_00000_v TINY-OBJECT SCENE', 'MOTA A0  0.2161 A3  0.4449 GAIN  +0.2287', 'IDF1 A0  0.3505 A3  0.5359 GAIN  +0.1854', 'Largest MOTA gain among all 17 sequences. A3 recovers many more tiny targets through adaptive resolution; this is the clearest direct A0-to-A3 example.', 'VIDEO: 12 seconds | 1920 x 1080 | A0 vs A3 only | recorded T4 run'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_59.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide59.xml'}, 60: {'id': 60, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VII', 'text': ['Implementation, Scale and Consistency', 'VII', 'The scale of the experimental work, and the measures that make every reported run reproducible.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_60.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide60.xml'}, 61: {'id': 61, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VII - REPRODUCIBILITY', 'text': ['Measures Taken to Ensure Consistency', '62', 'Same hardware for all Every system ran on the same Tesla T4 with batch 1. The predictor reported actual_model_fp16=False, so this run is labelled FP32. Like-for-like by construction.', 'Local SSD copy Sequences were copied to local disk before inference, so the FPS reading is not polluted by network delays.', 'Clean state each time model.predictor = None, The tracking state is reset before each video, so every video starts with fresh track IDs and does not inherit tracks from the previous video.', 'Per-sequence CSVs The run records tracks, settings, timing, per-sequence metrics, hashes, checkpoints and JSON sidecars for all 85 cases.', 'One fixed GT filter The same classes, the same exclusions, the same score rule - applied identically to all five systems.', 'The complete status, configuration, manifest and video index let the full run be audited without rerunning inference.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_61.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide61.xml'}, 62: {'id': 62, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VII - CODE MAP', 'text': ['Where Every Parameter Lives in the Code', '63'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_62.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide62.xml'}, 63: {'id': 63, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VIII', 'text': ['Scope, Conclusion and Future Work', 'VIII', 'The scope of the present study, its contribution, and the research directions that follow from the measurements.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_63.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide63.xml'}, 64: {'id': 64, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VIII - SCOPE', 'text': ['What We Focused On and Why', '65', 'The absolute MOTA is low - on purpose YOLOv8n is kept fixed so any improvement comes from our method, not a bigger detector. The calibrator can be used with other YOLO models later. The calibrator drops onto any YOLO in deployment.', 'One benchmark, one split We tested one benchmark split: all 17 VisDrone2019-MOT test-dev sequences, covering crowded, night, tiny-object and clear scenes. More datasets and splits will be tested later.', 'The ReID we tried was basic A basic grayscale crop-similarity cue. It provides no meaningful gain and increases IDS, so it is not adopted. Reported as A4. A compact deep embedding is the next step.', 'CONCLUSION We kept the detector fixed so that any improvement reflects the adaptive SCI strategy, not simply a larger model.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_64.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide64.xml'}, 65: {'id': 65, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VIII - CONCLUSION', 'text': ['Conclusion: What AC-MOT Delivers', '66', '+32.1% MOTA', '+21.2% IDF1', '+15.9% HOTA*', '-30% ID switches', '3.7x faster than BoT-SORT', 'What the thesis contributes ▸  Scene-adaptive calibration, with no retraining ▸  A simple and lightweight SCI that runs on CPU. ▸  Improvements are clearly measured using ablation tests.', 'Why it matters in practice ▸  Runs at 28.9 FPS on the recorded T4/FP32 setup ▸  Works with any YOLO without changing the tracker. ▸  Uses scene difficulty to adjust the system automatically.', 'E X A M P L E If the scene changes, the system should adapt automatically — without retraining and with very low cost.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_65.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide65.xml'}, 66: {'id': 66, 'type': 'section_slide', 'title': 'SECTION VIII - FUTURE WORK', 'text': ['Directions for Future Work', '67', '1. Identity-aware association The clearest next gain: it is exactly what gives BoT-SORT its 247 switches. Adding it could close the remaining gap while keeping our speed. AC-MOT 325 IDS  vs  BoT-SORT 247 IDS.', '2. Density-gated resolution In the two densest sequences the 832 px step costs recall. Gating resolution on density as well as SCI should recover it. Aimed at exactly s249a and s306.', '3. A proper deep ReID Replace the basic grayscale crop cue with a compact deep embedding suited to aerial viewpoints - light enough to keep real-time speed. Builds directly on configuration A4.', '4. SCI beyond YOLO Run the official VisDrone evaluator, then test the same SCI controller on UAVDT, AU-AIR and stronger detectors. Tests whether the idea generalises.', 'E X A M P L E These directions come directly from the full-17 evidence: A3 gives the largest accuracy step, A4 adds no meaningful benefit, and the identity conclusion changes with the IoU gate.'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_66.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide66.xml'}, 67: {'id': 67, 'type': 'results_slide', 'title': 'Thank you', 'text': ['AC-MOT — adaptive scene calibration for real-time UAV tracking.', '+32.1% MOTA', '+21.2% IDF1', '+15.9% HOTA*', '-30% ID switches', '23.9 FPS, live', 'IF YOU REMEMBER ONE THING The scene tells you how carefully to look. Measure it, and let the detector listen.', 'Ahmed Gouda Ismail   ·   Mohamed S. Mohamed   ·   Tarek Ahmed Mahmoud', 'Questions?'], 'background_image': 'assets/images/slide_67.png', 'source_slide_xml': 'ppt/slides/slide67.xml'}}
